A static HTML component produces markup. Its behaviour starts only
when the selected page's runtime code asks for it.

### Start with the browser features already available

Moth's documentation sidebar uses an explicit runtime call after its
static fragments:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    @styles sidebar, init_docs_sidebar

    [sidebar]

    init_docs_sidebar()!
]

The sidebar template doesn't run a script when it is imported or rendered.
Its initializer uses the existing `@web/page` browser package to
synchronise the active navigation link and breadcrumb.

A checkbox and CSS can manage the sidebar's simple open/closed state
without Moth receiving an event. Use browser-native HTML and CSS for
behaviour that needs no application logic.

### The intended DOM boundary

The planned `@web/dom` package will provide typed operations for finding
elements, reading properties and geometry, setting attributes and updating
the live document. Ordinary Moth functions will own the page-specific
algorithm.

[codeblock, $code "text":
    const component -> static HTML and tracked CSS
    @entry runtime -> explicit init function
    init function -> @web/dom operations
    browser DOM -> visible page update
]

For example, a future sidebar initializer can measure the navbar and set a
CSS custom property controlling the sidebar's top edge. That algorithm
belongs to the docs package, not the generic DOM binding.

This is a **future package boundary**, not a claim that `@web/dom` is
available today. The browser adapter will still use JavaScript internally.
Application code should not need to embed JavaScript source or script
listeners in its component templates.

### Later: event-driven updates

The intended `@web/events` package will connect browser events to typed
Moth messages and Channels. It will let a sidebar react to scroll, resize
and expansion without an authored JavaScript file. Until the Channel and
host-ingress runtime exists, that behaviour remains deferred.

See @../../packages/builder/page/ (@web/page) and
@../../wiring (Wiring).
